outputs:
Correct option: E. Level of unconsciousness  

Rationale: In a head-injured patient the standard bedside observations include  
• Vital signs – pulse, blood pressure, temperature, respiratory rate;  
• Fluid input/output – to detect dehydration, SIADH, diabetes insipidus, etc.;  
• Pupillary size and reaction – gives rapid information about brain-stem function and possible herniation;  
• Level of consciousness – regularly assessed with the Glasgow Coma Scale.  

“Level of unconsciousness” is not a separate or recognised parameter; it is already encompassed in the routine assessment of the patient’s level of consciousness with the GCS. Hence it is the only item in the list that is not essential as an independent measurement.
